Chapter: GpuPtx

  cites Kernel chapter GpuBridge
  cites AI chapter GpuProxy
  cites AI chapter Tensor
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 A kernel written in PTX runs on the actual graphics processor.

 Every other operation the compute bridge serves is computed by the
 emulator on the host CPU, in scalar C. This one is not: the module below
 is handed to the CUDA driver, compiled for whatever card is present, and
 launched. That is the difference between a bridge that dispatches and a
 bridge that pretends to.

 THE ARITHMETIC IS CHOSEN SO THAT NOTHING ELSE COULD HAVE PRODUCED IT.
 The kernel computes ten times the input plus five, which no operation in
 the host's scalar table computes, over an input whose values are 1, 2, 3
 and 4. The answers are 15, 25, 35 and 45: four distinct numbers, none of
 them an input, none of them a permutation of the inputs, and none
 reachable by echoing the operand buffer back.

 TWO CONTROLS, AND THEY ARE THE POINT. A test that only ever sees the
 kernel succeed cannot tell a driver that compiled and ran the module
 from a host that answered a canned reply.

   The KERNEL NAME control asks the same good module for an entry point
   that is not in it. It must be refused. That is what proves the host
   resolves a function rather than launching whatever it has.

   The PTX control hands over text that is not a module at all. It must
   be refused. That is what proves an assembler actually read the bytes,
   which in turn is what proves the text survived the CCE boundary as
   ASCII rather than arriving as plausible-looking code points.

 A box with no NVIDIA GPU refuses all three, and this test then FAILS
 rather than skipping. That is deliberate: a skip reads as coverage, and
 the claim being made here is about hardware. A failure that names the
 missing device is the honest answer on a machine that does not have one.

 We say:

Section: The Module

 PTX is the portable form, so the target below is an old architecture on
 purpose: the driver compiles it forward for whatever card is present.
 The kernel takes the three parameters the bridge's ABI fixes -- operand
 pointer, result pointer, result count -- and guards on the count, so a
 grid rounded up to a whole block writes nothing past the end.

  ptx-head : Text
  ptx-head = ".version 6.0\n.target sm_50\n.address_size 64\n\n"

  ptx-entry : Text
  ptx-entry = ".visible .entry tenx5(\n.param .u64 p_in,\n.param .u64 p_out,\n.param .u32 p_n\n)\n{\n"

  ptx-regs : Text
  ptx-regs = ".reg .pred %p<2>;\n.reg .f32 %f<4>;\n.reg .b32 %r<6>;\n.reg .b64 %rd<8>;\n"

  ptx-index : Text
  ptx-index = "ld.param.u64 %rd1, [p_in];\nld.param.u64 %rd2, [p_out];\nld.param.u32 %r2, [p_n];\nmov.u32 %r3, %ctaid.x;\nmov.u32 %r4, %ntid.x;\nmov.u32 %r5, %tid.x;\nmad.lo.s32 %r1, %r3, %r4, %r5;\n"

  ptx-guard : Text
  ptx-guard = "setp.ge.s32 %p1, %r1, %r2;\n@%p1 bra DONE;\n"

  ptx-body : Text
  ptx-body = "cvta.to.global.u64 %rd3, %rd1;\nmul.wide.s32 %rd4, %r1, 4;\nadd.s64 %rd5, %rd3, %rd4;\nld.global.f32 %f1, [%rd5];\nmul.f32 %f2, %f1, 0f41200000;\nadd.f32 %f3, %f2, 0f40A00000;\n"

  ptx-store : Text
  ptx-store = "cvta.to.global.u64 %rd6, %rd2;\nadd.s64 %rd7, %rd6, %rd4;\nst.global.f32 [%rd7], %f3;\nDONE:\nret;\n}\n"

  ptx-tenx5 : Text
  ptx-tenx5 =
    let a = ptx-head & ptx-entry
    in let b = ptx-regs & ptx-index
    in let c = ptx-guard & ptx-body
    in a & b & c & ptx-store

 Not a module. The driver must reject it.

  ptx-rubbish : Text
  ptx-rubbish = "this is not ptx and never was\n"

Section: Operands

  bridge : GpuBridge
  bridge = gpu-bridge-init

  quad : Tensor
  quad = Tensor { data = [1000, 2000, 3000, 4000], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

Section: Tolerance

 The guest decoder divides the mantissa down to scale 1000 before it
 applies the exponent, so it loses low bits on any value above 1.0. These
 assertions allow one part in a hundred, which is nowhere near the 10000
 units that separate two neighbouring answers here.

  near : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  near (got) (want) =
    let diff = if got > want then got - want else want - got
    in let slack = if want > 0 then want / 100 else 100
    in diff <= slack

Section: Reports

  launch-report : Maybe Tensor -> Text
  launch-report (m) =
    when m
     is None -> "NO GPU OR REFUSED"
     is Just (t) -> launch-values t

  launch-values : Tensor -> Text
  launch-values (t) =
    if list-length (t.data) /= 4 then "count " & show (list-length (t.data))
    else if near (list-at (t.data) 0) 15000
     & near (list-at (t.data) 1) 25000
     & near (list-at (t.data) 2) 35000
     & near (list-at (t.data) 3) 45000
    then "ok"
    else "wrong " & show (list-at (t.data) 0) & " " & show (list-at (t.data) 1) & " "
       & show (list-at (t.data) 2) & " " & show (list-at (t.data) 3)

 A refusal is the wanted answer for both controls, so it is reported as
 such rather than as a failure.

  refusal-report : Maybe Tensor -> Text
  refusal-report (m) =
    when m
     is None -> "refused"
     is Just (t) -> "SERVED " & show (list-length (t.data))

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Integer
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("launch: " & launch-report (gpu-bridge-launch-ptx bridge ptx-tenx5 "tenx5" quad 2 2 64))
    print-line-uni ("wrong-name: " & refusal-report (gpu-bridge-launch-ptx bridge ptx-tenx5 "nosuchkernel" quad 2 2 64))
    print-line-uni ("bad-ptx: " & refusal-report (gpu-bridge-launch-ptx bridge ptx-rubbish "tenx5" quad 2 2 64))
    let r = 0 in r
  end
